index 4889799c8..e49039818 100644
--- a/server/etcdserver/api/v3rpc/CODEMANIFEST
+++ b/server/etcdserver/api/v3rpc/CODEMANIFEST
@@ -18,13 +18,36 @@ Usages:
     term, and revision by a shared header helper. Add a new cross-cutting gRPC-level concern as
     a new decorator around the relevant service struct rather than editing it directly.
 
+    This pattern only fires for the specific methods a decorator chooses to override (see the
+    quota decorator on the KV service, which overrides Put/Txn but not Range/DeleteRange) — it
+    embeds the wrapped service, so an un-overridden method silently falls through to the base
+    implementation. It is the right tool when a concern intentionally applies to a subset of
+    methods, but the wrong tool when a concern must apply uniformly to every method of a service
+    including ones added later. For that case use `uniform_service_audit` instead.
+
+  uniform_service_audit: |
+    A concern that must cover every method of a gRPC service uniformly, including methods added
+    to that service in the future, is implemented as a unary interceptor filtered by gRPC service
+    name (full-method prefix, e.g. the KV service's) rather than as a per-service-struct decorator
+    that overrides individual methods by name. The interceptor is wired into the fixed internal
+    interceptor chain composed inside `Server`, not its optional external interceptor
+    parameter — which callers may leave nil — so it always runs. It observes the already-flowing request/response/error and
+    only logs; it never mutates them, so it cannot change functional behavior or response content.
+    The audit-trail interceptor is the instance of this pattern: it logs identity, operation kind,
+    key/range, duration, and outcome for every KV Range/Put/DeleteRange/Txn call, deriving
+    operation kind and key/range generically (by method-name suffix and by the request message's
+    key/range-end field convention) so a future KV method is still audited, with a best-effort
+    label, without editing this cell.
+
 Annotations: |
   This cell is the gRPC-facing adapter layer: it translates protobuf service calls into calls
   on the imported `EtcdServer`, plus a direct dependency on `KV` for bridging gRPC watch streams
   to the storage layer's watch mechanism. It is the top of the server-side request path — the
   client cell talks to it over the network, and `EtcdServer` itself never imports back from it.
 
-  Use `decorator_wrapping` when adding a new cross-cutting concern to an existing gRPC service.
+  Use `decorator_wrapping` when adding a new cross-cutting concern to a specific subset of an
+  existing gRPC service's methods. Use `uniform_service_audit` when the concern must cover every
+  method of a service uniformly and automatically, without per-method instrumentation.
 
 ---
 
